Subject: Expose native Wasm tag identities and catchable exceptions through wee8

Base: V8 245ce17ed8483e6bc3de7f668635f46c5bfdd8a0 (rusty_v8 0.106.0),
after wasm-c-api.patch. No generated code or source lists are changed.

The pinned C API aborts when describing an imported/exported tag, even though
V8 executes exception handling internally. Implement the upstream header's
TagType declarations, add runtime tag handles and casts, and enable the pinned
experimental_wasm_exnref flag before engine initialization for try_table and
throw_ref. Tag identities use WasmExceptionTag and the existing canonical type
registry, exactly as V8's WebAssembly.Tag constructor does; equal signatures
never make independently created tags equal.

The wasm_tag_* runtime functions and wee8_exception_* functions are wee8
extensions, not a claim that wasm-c-api standardizes exception transport.
Exceptions use the existing owned wasm_trap_t callback return channel, carrying
WasmExceptionPackage rather than an Error with wasm_uncatchable_symbol. The
existing FuncData trampoline already throws the retained native object. Thus
explicit guest exceptions are catchable, while wasm_trap_new host failures stay
uncatchable. No JavaScript source evaluation or new object representation is used.

Construction validates store identity, payload arity and kinds. Payloads support
scalar numbers and nullable externref. Reading requires the matching tag and
returns independently owned reference roots. Unsupported payload types return
NULL/false; import/export metadata keeps the C API's existing scalar/nullable
reference limit. The Rust validator rejects unsupported metadata before calling
native introspection. GC values, v128 and exception references are not exposed
as host payload values. Tags and exceptions must be destroyed before their store.

The packaged C consumer checks import/export metadata, tag identity, matching
and wrong-tag catches, catch_all, guest throws, throw_ref, payloads, ordinary
host failure behavior, and continued execution. Candidate Rust tests additionally
check reference rooting/finalization, start-section exceptions, cross-store and
stale handles, malformed payloads, callback panics, pending exception cleanup,
serialization and unsupported boundary rejection. Published-release tests still
use the old ABI; the opt-in exceptions feature requires this candidate header
and archive until a native release is published.

Review/remove this patch when upgrading to a V8 C API providing equivalent tag
and exception support. It does not add Moonrun's test driver or host imports.

